\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Quality--style confounding and construct validity.}
The NLI gate blocks content changes but cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself---a fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches.
Although the formality effect survives logistic adjustment for original preference ($\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$; Appendix~\ref{app:direction}), residual confounding is not fully eliminated.
An alternative interpretation of the Blindspot is that Likert and pairwise modes measure different constructs: Likert captures absolute quality difference while pairwise captures ordinal preference. Under this view, the divergence reflects construct difference rather than measurement failure. The Comparative Trap \citep{jeong2025comparative} lends support to this alternative; six experimental controls bound this risk (\S\ref{sec:amplification}), though definitive separation would require human ground-truth ratings.

\paragraph{Evaluator-producer bias and generalizability.}
Qwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge, raising self-preference concerns \citep{panickssery2024selfpreference}; an independent GPT-4o rewriter bounds this to a 22\% Style OR reduction (Appendix~\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).
GPT-4o, Llama-3.3-70B, and Gemini-2.5-Flash use a separate rewriter and are not subject to evaluator-producer overlap; all three independently confirm the Blindspot across four architectures, though cross-judge effect-size comparisons are not valid.
Register results carry the highest evaluator-producer risk---all register rewrites use the same Qwen3-32B rewriter without the cross-rewriter validation applied to formality and verbosity---and register conclusions remain tentative pending independent rewriter validation.
All experiments use LLMBar ($n = 100$); additional axes, larger benchmarks, and further judges are needed.
A fourth axis---tone (assertive vs.\ hedging)---was explored but abandoned: epistemic commitment changes caused the NLI gate to correctly reject most counterfactuals (pass rate ${<}50\%$), confirming that the quality gate prevents content-altering manipulations from entering the pipeline.
Our per-axis decomposition treats each style axis independently; in practice, axes may interact (e.g., formality shifts may co-vary with register), and such confounds are not isolated by the current design.

\paragraph{Validation methodology and scale design.}
NLI validation uses Qwen3-32B as annotator (GPT-4o cross-validation on all 200 pairs confirms 92.1\% formality / 82.6\% verbosity pass precision); human evaluation of both original and rewritten texts would strengthen content-preservation claims.
A 100-point continuous scale does not resolve the Blindspot (Appendix~\ref{app:likert100}), suggesting the gap reflects measurement mode rather than granularity \citep{preston2000scale}; equivalence testing \citep{lakens2017equivalence} could formalize whether the Likert effect size is practically zero.

\section*{Code and Data}
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

