\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}


\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

We designate a primary family of six hypothesis tests (three judges $\times$ two axes: formality and verbosity) and control the family-wise error rate with Holm-Bonferroni correction at $\alpha = 0.05$.
Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports both raw and adjusted $p$-values.
Analyses outside this grid are classified as either \emph{exploratory} (NLI threshold sensitivity sweep, \S\ref{sec:nli_threshold}) or \emph{confirmatory} (consistency filter validation, \S\ref{sec:consistency}).
Exploratory analyses are reported with uncorrected $p$-values and interpreted as hypothesis-generating; confirmatory analyses test specific hypotheses about pipeline validity and are evaluated at the nominal $\alpha = 0.05$ level without additional correction, as they address orthogonal questions about measurement reliability rather than the primary style-bias family.
Bradley-Terry parameters are estimated via spectral initialization followed by minorization-maximization \citep{hunter2004mm}, using the default regularization of the \texttt{choix} Python library.
All bootstrap confidence intervals use 10{,}000 pair-level resamples with seed 42.

\paragraph{Model selection.}
Target judges span four architectures: GPT-4o (2024-08-06), the dominant judge powering AlpacaEval and MT-Bench leaderboards; Qwen3-32B-Instruct, also serving as rewriter for methodological consistency; Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct, the latest dense Llama model (Llama~4's MoE architecture introduces an additional confound); and Gemini-2.5-Flash as a confirmatory fourth architecture. Claude Sonnet~4 serves as negative control.
We pin versions for reproducibility; CSD applies to any black-box judge.

