\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}


\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}
\label{sec:nli_validation}

\paragraph{False negative rate.}
Validating all 100 NLI decisions against an LLM annotator (Qwen3-32B) yields 91/100 agreement (Gwet's AC1 $= 0.89$; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.43$, depressed by prevalence skew \citep{feinstein1990kappa}), with FNR $= 8.4\%$ (8 of 12 rejected pairs judged content-preserving).
Since this annotator shares the rewriter model, the FNR may be optimistic; GPT-4o cross-validation below provides model-independent verification.
Three robustness checks confirm these do not compromise conclusions: BT stable across 11 NLI thresholds (${\leq}3$pp variation; \S\ref{sec:nli_threshold}), no covariate differences between retained and discarded pairs ($p > 0.12$; \S\ref{sec:consistency}), and no directional bias in false negatives (Fisher $p = 0.35$).
The gate operates as a precision-oriented filter (precision $= 98.9\%$); verbosity-rejected pairs have larger edit distances (Cliff's $\delta = +0.45$, $p < 0.001$), making estimates lower bounds.
Full-scale GPT-4o validation on all 200 decisions yields 92.1\% formality / 82.6\% verbosity pass precision, the lower verbosity figure reflecting direction-aware gating permissiveness (17.4\% vs.\ 7.9\% FPR).
Admitted false positives would inflate verbosity BT only if content-leaked pairs systematically favor the styled variant; the GEE originality OR ${<}1$ indicates the opposite direction, making the estimate a conservative lower bound.

\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}
\label{sec:nli_threshold}
A sweep across 11 NLI thresholds (0.50--0.98) confirms robustness: all 44 judge$\times$axis$\times$threshold combinations are significant (${\leq}3$pp variation; Appendix~\ref{app:nli_sweep}), and the bimodal NLI score distribution places the 0.90 threshold on a plateau.


\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\ Pairwise}
\label{sec:likert_pairwise}

\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}
Applying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\% formal preference ($p = 0.0004$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).
This is the Likert Blindspot: the same data, the same style intervention, two measurement instruments---one detects bias, the other does not.
A $4\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) distinguishes measurement attenuation from insufficient power: the Likert effect size shrinks to $d = {-}0.025$ (near zero) while BT remains stable at $67.5\%$ ($p < 0.0001$)---if the gap were merely statistical power, $d$ would remain non-zero at larger $n$.
Bootstrap subsampling confirms: $d$ remains flat near zero ($d_{\text{median}} = {-}0.017$) as CIs narrow from ${\pm}0.3$ to ${\pm}0.1$, never excluding zero (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_attenuation}).
Attenuation follows a severity spectrum: at moderate bias (BT 63--67\%), it is complete ($d \to 0$ at $n = 355$); at stronger effects (BT ${>}70\%$), Likert partially detects but attenuates ${>}60\%$ (MT-Bench $d \approx 0.15$ for BT ${\sim}70\%$)---explaining why three of eleven bias-positive cells escape the Blindspot (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, $\ddagger$ rows).

The frameworks are complementary: RATE measures aggregate quality via Likert ATE; CSD targets per-axis style preference via forced-choice comparison, eliminating ties.
The Blindspot arises because Likert tie rates cluster at 58--73\% across all nine configurations; a 100-point continuous scale does not resolve this ($p = 0.725$/$0.117$; Appendix~\ref{app:likert100}), confirming a paradigm difference rather than granularity limitation.


\paragraph{Sensitivity hierarchy across measurement methods.}
To confirm that the Likert Blindspot is not an artifact of a particular statistical test, we apply seven measurement methods to the same formality data and rank them by sensitivity (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}).
Both pairwise methods detect the formality preference; all Likert-based methods fail regardless of whether they assume interval scaling (ATE $t$-test), ordinal scaling (ordinal logistic regression), or nonparametric ranking (Wilcoxon signed-rank).
The same attenuation pattern replicates on verbosity (Appendix~\ref{app:verb_sensitivity}), where all six Likert methods remain non-significant despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\%, $p < 0.001$).

