\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:power_curve} plots detection probability curves at observed effect magnitudes (not post-hoc power; cf.\ \citealt{hoenig2001abuse}): Likert requires ${\approx}4.5\times$ more data to detect the same bias.
Formal power analysis across all six judge$\times$axis cells confirms: Likert would require $4$--$206\times$ the current sample size ($336$--$27{,}159$ pairs) to detect effects that BT identifies at $p < 0.001$ with $n \leq 100$; for formality, the required samples exceed $18{,}000$ pairs---orders of magnitude beyond typical evaluation budgets (Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}).
\paragraph{Measurement attenuation vs.\ construct divergence.}
An alternative interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure different constructs---absolute quality vs.\ ordinal preference---making the divergence a construct difference rather than measurement failure.
\citet{tripathi2025pairwise} independently confirm that pairwise and pointwise protocols exhibit distinct sensitivity profiles, with pairwise preferences being more volatile---consistent with this construct divergence interpretation.
Both interpretations lead to the same conclusion: Likert audits miss style preferences that drive pairwise-based alignment pipelines (RLHF, DPO).

\paragraph{Detection vs.\ amplification.}
\label{sec:amplification}
\citet{jeong2025comparative} show that pairwise comparison amplifies style biases (the Comparative Trap).
Format-level amplification cannot be fully excluded, but six independent controls bound this risk: (i)~NLI gate ablation reverses NLI-fail pairs to BT 41.7\% (Appendix~\ref{app:nli_ablation}); (ii)~Claude Sonnet 4 shows no bias with the identical pairwise format (BT 56.2\%/54.1\%, n.s.); (iii)~cross-axis BT variation (register 55\% $<$ formality 67\% $<$ verbosity 77\%) is inconsistent with uniform format amplification; (iv)~rewriting artifact absorbed by GEE decomposition (Appendix~\ref{app:artifact_control}); (v)~debiased prompting partially reduces bias (verbosity $-13.1$pp; formality $-5.7$/$-0.4$pp); (vi)~four independent architectures all confirm the Blindspot with consistent GEE confound structure (\S\ref{sec:cross_judge}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/power_curve.pdf}
\caption{Detection probability at observed effect magnitudes. 80\% power crossings: sign test $n \approx 62$, BT $n \approx 114$, binarized Likert $n \approx 462$, Likert ATE $n \approx 1{,}473$. At typical scales ($n = 50$--$200$, shaded), pairwise methods reach 80\% power while Likert methods remain far below. At $n = 355$, Likert $d = {-}0.025$ confirms the gap is structural, not merely a power issue.}
\label{fig:power_curve}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication}
\label{sec:cross_judge}

Three independent judges replicate the Blindspot: Qwen3-32B (BT 66.5\%, $p < 0.0001$; Likert $p = 0.908$), Llama-3.3-70B (BT 69.5\%, $p = 0.0001$; Likert $p = 0.846$), and Gemini-2.5-Flash (confirmatory: BT 70.8\%, $p < 0.0001$; Likert $d = 0.05$, n.s.).
Gemini's formality confound is originality-dominated (Style OR $= 1.64$, n.s.; Orig OR $= 2.51$, $p = 0.002$), unlike the other three judges (\S\ref{sec:direction}).

\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}
\label{sec:cross_axis}

Verbosity provides the strongest Blindspot: all four target judges show consistent preference (GPT-4o 76.0\%, Qwen3-32B 77.3\%, Llama-70B 73.5\%, Gemini 70.8\%; all $p \leq 0.0001$), while Likert ATE remains non-significant for all ($p = 0.179$--$0.752$).
GEE confirms massive style effects (OR $8.2$--$13.1$, all $p < .0001$) with originality ORs below 1 ($0.38$--$0.49$), indicating artifacts favor rewrites rather than reflecting quality confounding (Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
Both rewriting directions favor verbose responses (90.7\%/80.6\%); controlling for length leaves the style OR unchanged ($<$2\%).
Evaluator-producer overlap applies only to Qwen3-32B; an independent GPT-4o rewriter bounds this to a $22\%$ Style OR reduction ($13.10 \to 10.17$; Appendix~\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).

\subsection{Register: Judge-Dependent Confound Structure}
\label{sec:negative_control}

Register shows judge-dependent heterogeneity (Llama-70B BT 63.5\%, GPT-4o 57.0\%, Qwen3-32B n.s.; all Likert n.s.) with varying GEE confound structure (Table~\ref{tab:gee}); classified as exploratory outside the primary family.

\subsection{GEE Conditional Decomposition and Confound Structure}
\label{sec:direction}

GEE conditional decomposition \citep{liang1986gee} with exchangeable working correlation and robust standard errors separates style from originality effects (Appendix~\ref{app:direction}; model diagnostics including correlation structure robustness and interaction tests in Appendix~\ref{app:gee_diagnostics}).
After controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant (GPT-4o: OR $= 2.58$, $p = 0.002$; Qwen3-32B: OR $= 2.15$, $p < 0.0001$, $n = 355$; Llama-70B: OR $= 4.81$, $p < 0.0001$; Table~\ref{tab:gee}); the significant originality ORs confirm residual confounding.
The $4\times$ replication ($n = 355$) confirms the Blindspot: BT $67.5\%$ (halved CI), Likert n.s.\ ($p = 0.60$).

