The confound structure is consistent across all four target judges for verbosity (style-dominated: Style OR $8.2$--$13.1$, Orig OR $0.38$--$0.49$); formality is conditionally style-driven in three judges but originality-dominated in Gemini; register is judge-dependent.
Cross-benchmark replication confirms formality bias across all six benchmark$\times$judge configurations (BT 63--73\%, all $p < 0.001$); Claude Sonnet 4 shows no detectable bias (BT 56.2\%/54.1\%, n.s.; TOST equivalence at $\Delta = 15$pp, Appendix~\ref{app:claude_tost}).
The Blindspot manifests in eight of eleven bias-positive cells; three exceptions (BT ${>}69\%$, $d \geq 0.14$) confirm it is effect-size dependent.
Same-style double-rewrite controls confirm a rewriting artifact (Appendix~\ref{app:artifact_control}), absorbed by GEE's originality term.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

CSD, a black-box framework, yields two findings across five judges, three benchmarks, and three style axes.
First, the \emph{Likert Blindspot}: Likert scoring systematically misses style biases that pairwise comparison detects---complete at moderate bias ($d \to 0$ at $n = 355$), ${>}60\%$ attenuation at stronger effects---confirmed by seven statistical methods; whether this reflects measurement attenuation or construct divergence, Likert-only audits miss style-driven preferences that propagate through alignment pipelines.
Second, GEE conditional decomposition reveals per-axis confound structure---verbosity style-dominated, formality conditional, register judge-dependent---determining which estimates require conditional analysis.
Existing Likert-based null findings may reflect measurement insensitivity; pairwise comparison with amplification controls (\S\ref{sec:amplification}) offers superior detection sensitivity.

